**Diary — 2026-03-14 — Porting the CUDA engine to HIP on gfx906**

Today I moved the core CUDA engine onto ROCm/HIP targeting `gfx906` (Vega 10 / MI50/MI60 class). The goal was not a toy demo: I wanted the existing kernel pipeline to compile, run, and produce bit-compatible-enough results on AMD.

### 1. Build setup

I started from the CUDA source tree and introduced a HIP build path.

CUDA:
```bash
nvcc -O3 -arch=sm_75 -o engine_cuda engine.cu
```

HIP:
```bash
hipcc -O3 --offload-arch=gfx906 -o engine_hip engine.hip
```

I renamed `.cu` to `.hip` and kept the CUDA-style kernel syntax because HIP accepts it:

```cpp
__global__ void reduce_kernel(float* out, const float* in, int n) {
    __shared__ float smem[256];

    int tid = threadIdx.x;
    int idx = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + tid;

    float v = (idx < n) ? in[idx] : 0.0f;

    smem[tid] = v;
    __syncthreads();

    for (int s = blockDim.x / 2; s > 0; s >>= 1) {
        if (tid < s) {
            smem[tid] += smem[tid + s];
        }
        __syncthreads();
    }

    if (tid == 0) {
        atomicAdd(out, smem[0]);
    }
}
```

That compiled cleanly with `hipcc`.

